      }
    ],
    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Stop if you feel sharp, radiating or nerve-type pain.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG.",
        "Low-back pain, disc problems or flexion intolerance: start with a McGill-style curl-up, dead bug or plank; partial curl-ups gave the best abdominal challenge for the least spine compression in one model study (Axler and McGill 1997).",
        "Hip or groin pain in the frog position: move the feet further from the hips or use a pillow under each knee.",
        "Pregnancy and postpartum (diastasis recti): get individual guidance before dynamic trunk-flexion work.",
        "Osteoporosis or vertebral fracture: avoid repeated loaded spinal flexion and twisting.",
        "The athlete mesh rests on the mat; the rig collision shapes are thicker than the mesh at the back and buttocks, so floorTolerance records how far those proxy shapes sink below the mat surface."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Neck pain: cross the arms on the chest.",
        "Tight hips: feet further away, knees less open."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Smaller range: head and shoulders only.",
        "Crunch with feet flat."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Slow 3 s lowering with a 2 s hold.",
        "Arms reaching overhead."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "core": "Rectus abdominis curls the ribs toward the pelvis exactly as in a crunch (about 53% upper and 39% lower rectus MVIC, Escamilla 2016). The legs only change the base.",
      "obliques_r": "Assist the curl and keep the trunk square (about 28-33% MVIC in a crunch, Escamilla 2016).",
      "obliques_l": "Assist the curl and keep the trunk square (about 28-33% MVIC in a crunch, Escamilla 2016).",
      "adductors_r": "Lengthened with the knees out; they rest, which is why the frog position is used to take the legs out of the movement (clinical opinion). Hip flexors stay quiet in pure trunk-flexion work (Andersson 1997).",
      "adductors_l": "Lengthened with the knees out; they rest, which is why the frog position is used to take the legs out of the movement (clinical opinion). Hip flexors stay quiet in pure trunk-flexion work (Andersson 1997)."
    }
  },
